In article <91156.210650CERLPDR@TECHNION.BITNET> CERLPDR@TECHNION.BITNET writes:
>      With all the constant talk of stemming NH abuse I'm surprised no one
>   has ever complained about the ability of an athame to engrave Elbereth
>   indefinitely. I recently began to play wizards and I was quite impressed
>   by the power of this feature. Properly executed I found it a lot more
>   destabilizing than polypiling or a ring of conflict, especially in the
>   early game where survival is most difficult. Any comments?

I can't say I agree. Although the engraving is always right first time
every time (unless you are blind) it still does scuff out after a while.
So you can't reliably fight on top of it ... if you choose to start
swiping at monsters and the engraving does scuff, you can't re-engrave at that
place, so it's quite easy to engrave yourself into a corner (especially in
corridors). This means that engraving isn't automatic salvation for wizards.
Where it *is* useful is when you want to rest and regain hitpoints, which
is important (even necessary) given the wimpy stats of wizards.

What *does* seem to unbalance the game a bit, however, is an athame in
combination with a ring of levitation. Once you have these two in your
possession, you are almost certain to kill any monster with minimal risk
to yourself, especially if you have reflection (gotta watch out for
that dragon breath!) -- only minotaurs and humans present any real risk.
Combined with teleport control, which wizards get intrinsically, the
only real danger is running into a floating eye or drowning. Ho hum.

